---
/**
 * 2026-ballot.astro — the 2026 council-structure ballot explainer.
 *
 * ⚠ DRAFT copy — a live political topic. Updated Aug 2026 to reflect the two competing
 * charter amendments (the earlier single-vs-hybrid framing is obsolete). Kept nonpartisan
 * and dated; MUST be verified against the certified ballot language before the draft banner
 * is removed. Every substantive claim should trace to a primary source.
 */
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const b = base.endsWith("/") ? base : base + "/";
---
<Layout
  title="How Columbus elects its City Council is on the 2026 ballot"
  description="A nonpartisan explainer of the 2026 Columbus ballot measures on how City Council is elected — the citizen single-member-district amendment and City Council's 13-member expansion."
  ribbon={false}
>
  <div class="draftbar">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0Z"/></svg>
    <span><strong>Draft &mdash; pending certified ballot language.</strong> This is a developing, time-sensitive topic; the exact measures and wording are being verified against the certified ballot and may change.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap"><div class="article">
    <section class="art-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">Ballot Measures &middot; General Election, November 4, 2026</div>
      <h1>How Columbus elects its City Council is on the ballot</h1>
      <p class="dek">This November, Columbus voters are expected to weigh <strong>two competing charter amendments</strong> that would change how City Council is elected &mdash; one from a citizen petition, one from Council itself. They take different paths from the same starting point: today's nine members each represent a district but are elected by the whole city.</p>
      <div class="asof"><span class="dot"></span> As of August 2026 &middot; details may change before the ballot is certified</div>
      <div class="keyfacts">
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">What's on the ballot</div><div class="kfv">Two proposed charter amendments</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">How they qualified</div><div class="kfv">One by citizen petition, one referred by Council</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">When you vote</div><div class="kfv">Tuesday, November 4, 2026</div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="callout"><b>This is a developing story.</b> A compromise between the two sides was floated and then rejected, and Council has advanced its own amendment alongside the citizen petition. What ultimately appears on the ballot &mdash; and its exact wording &mdash; may still change. This page will be updated as the ballot is finalized; every claim should be checked against a primary source.</div>

    <h2>The system today</h2>
    <p>Columbus uses a <strong>hybrid</strong> system, in place since the 2023 elections. There are nine council members, and each must <strong>live in the district they represent</strong> &mdash; but every voter in the city votes in <strong>all nine</strong> district races. So members represent a district geographically while answering to the whole city. Both amendments below would change that arrangement in different ways.</p>

    <h2>The two measures, side by side</h2>
    <div class="compare2">
      <div class="sys prop">
        <div class="sh"><div class="lab">Citizen petition &middot; Our City Our Say</div><div class="nm">True single-member districts</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">What it changes</div><div class="sa">Keeps nine districts and the current map, but switches to district-only elections.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">How you'd vote</div><div class="sa">Only residents of your district vote for your representative &mdash; one council race on your ballot.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Council size</div><div class="sa">Stays at nine members.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Status</div><div class="sa">Qualified by petition &mdash; the Franklin County Board of Elections validated 15,945 signatures, above the required threshold.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sys today">
        <div class="sh"><div class="lab">Referred by City Council</div><div class="nm">A 13-member expanded council</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">What it changes</div><div class="sa">Expands council to 13 members &mdash; nine district seats plus four elected at-large &mdash; and calls for the district map to be redrawn in 2028.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">How you'd vote</div><div class="sa">You'd vote for your district's seat and for the at-large seats. (Exact election method is set by the amendment.)</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Council size</div><div class="sa">Grows from nine to 13 members.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Status</div><div class="sa">Advanced by City Council in August 2026 as a competing charter amendment.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.86rem;color:var(--ink-3);">The exact ballot titles, wording, and effective dates are set by the certified measures. Confirm details with the <a href="https://boe.franklincountyohio.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin County Board of Elections</a>.</p>

    <h2>How we got here</h2>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2018</div><div class="ty">Voters approve districts</div><div class="td">Columbus ends its century-old fully at-large council, adopting the hybrid district system and expanding council from seven seats to nine.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2023</div><div class="ty">First district elections</div><div class="td">All nine district seats are filled for the first time under the new system.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2025</div><div class="ty">Second cycle</div><div class="td">Districts 1, 3, 4, and 7 are up; the staggered four-year terms take hold.</div></div>
      <div class="tl now"><div class="tdate">Summer 2026</div><div class="ty">A petition qualifies</div><div class="td">The group Our City Our Say submits about 23,000 signatures for single-member districts; the Board of Elections validates 15,945 &mdash; enough to reach the ballot.</div></div>
      <div class="tl now"><div class="tdate">August 2026</div><div class="ty">Compromise rejected; a second measure</div><div class="td">Talks on a middle-ground deal collapse. City Council advances its own amendment &mdash; a 13-member council with four at-large seats and a 2028 remap &mdash; to run alongside the petition.</div></div>
      <div class="tl future"><div class="tdate">November 4, 2026</div><div class="ty">Columbus votes</div><div class="td">Voters decide the measures that appear on the certified ballot.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>What each side says</h2>
    <div class="procon">
      <div class="pc for">
        <h3>Arguments for changing the system</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Single-member districts give neighborhoods a representative who answers to them directly, not the whole city.</li>
          <li>Running for one district costs less than a citywide campaign, lowering the barrier to entry.</li>
          <li>Supporters of expansion say adding at-large seats keeps a citywide voice while growing representation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="pc against">
        <h3>Concerns and counterpoints</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Electing every member citywide keeps the whole council accountable to all of Columbus.</li>
          <li>Some labor and community leaders have raised concerns about how the changes could affect representation.</li>
          <li>The current system is only two cycles old &mdash; too soon to judge, some argue.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.86rem;color:var(--ink-3);">The points above summarize positions publicly stated by various parties; they are not endorsements. Columbus Districts is a nonpartisan reference and does not take a position on either measure.</p>

    <h2>Make your plan to vote</h2>
    <div class="vote-cta">
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 1</div><div class="vv">Check your registration</div><a href="https://www.ohiosos.gov/elections/voters/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ohio Secretary of State &rarr;</a></div>
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 2</div><div class="vv">Find your district</div><a href={`${b}all-districts/`}>Look up your address &rarr;</a></div>
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 3</div><div class="vv">Know the key dates</div><a href="https://boe.franklincountyohio.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin County BOE &rarr;</a></div>
    </div>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
